+/// sized for every level (MapSizeBytes, converted_size_bytes), though the dropped levels are never
+/// decoded or uploaded. Ocarina of Time's two 1365x1365x90 ASTC arrays keep levels 1+ (53 and 26
+/// MiB on the GPU) and asked for 214 + 107 MiB of output and 2 x 54 MiB of input at the first 3D
+/// scene, where the pool fell to 32 MiB. 1: count only (per decode and in the 10 s line, stderr),
+/// sizes as before. 2: read the guest from the first kept level, unswizzle only the kept levels
+/// (UnswizzleImage's first_level, as UploadLayers does) and size the output to their converted
+/// layout. 3: verify, for one test run: the old way decodes and uploads; the kept levels are also
+/// unswizzled and decoded the new way, and both inputs and both outputs are compared byte for byte
+/// (costs the kept size once more for input and output). Off, QueueAsyncDecode is today's.
